PUBLIC RESPONSE: C

The circumflex accent (ˆ) entered French orthography in the 16th–17th centuries, modeled on the ancient Greek circumflex and on the Latin “tilde” abbreviation. Its attested uses are therefore limited to the following historically documented functions:

- marking the site of a lost Latin /s/ (or other sibilant) that had become silent in Old French (H);  
- signaling the contraction of two vowels that had formerly been in hiatus (I) or the monophthongization of an earlier diphthong (G);  
- indicating a phonemically or historically long vowel, thereby contrasting length or timbre with the corresponding short/plain vowel (A, D);  
- disambiguating homophones that would otherwise be spelled identically (B);  
- supplying an etymological or merely decorative diacritic intended to give a word a more “Latin” or learned appearance (F).

Option C does not correspond to any of these documented motivations. While a handful of Latin words containing ō or ŭ later appear in French with ô (rôle < rotulus, etc.), the circumflex in those cases is explained by one of the functions already listed (loss of an intervening consonant, vowel contraction, or analogy); there is no independent orthographic convention that simply “marks a vowel descending from Classical Latin ō.” Hence C has never been an attested function of the accent.

